WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.029 --> 00:00:14.193
Tom Lindqvist: Hey Michelle, Brandon. Thanks for jumping on. I know we had this on the calendar but appreciate you making time on a Friday.

2
00:00:14.193 --> 00:00:16.845
Tom Lindqvist: So, How's Denver? Getting cold yet?

3
00:00:17.612 --> 00:00:23.860
Michelle Tran: It snowed Tuesday. Not a lot, but enough that everyone forgot how to drive. How are you?

4
00:00:24.502 --> 00:00:35.436
Tom Lindqvist: Good, good. Busy week. Before we get into it, I've got Lena Brooks on from the PreSense product team and Derek Shaw from support escalations.

5
00:00:35.436 --> 00:00:40.684
Tom Lindqvist: Lena, Derek, this is uh, Michelle Tran and Brandon Hughes from Northwind.

6
00:00:41.758 --> 00:00:44.401
Lena Brooks: Hi both. Thanks for the time.

7
00:00:45.755 --> 00:00:52.787
Derek Shaw: Hey. I've been reading through the tickets from the last couple weeks, so I'm roughly up to speed.

8
00:00:53.195 --> 00:00:56.667
Brandon Hughes: Appreciate you both being here. Let's get into it.

9
00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:59.710
Tom Lindqvist: Michelle, you want to kick us off?

10
00:01:00.594 --> 00:01:10.593
Michelle Tran: Yeah. So, short version. Since the Genesys update on the third, we're losing PreSense on basically every warm transfer. Not some. Every one.

11
00:01:11.975 --> 00:01:19.166
Lena Brooks: Okay. When you say losing PreSense, what does the agent actually see? Blank screen pop, wrong data, nothing at all?

12
00:01:20.266 --> 00:01:30.104
Michelle Tran: Nothing. The screen pop just doesn't fire. Caller gets to the second agent, the Genesys window comes up, and there's no PreSense panel.

13
00:01:30.104 --> 00:01:32.671
Michelle Tran: No journey, no ad source, nothing.

14
00:01:33.598 --> 00:01:40.167
Lena Brooks: And on a cold transfer, so a straight transfer with no consult, does it fire?

15
00:01:40.805 --> 00:01:51.251
Michelle Tran: Cold transfers are fine. That's the weird part. It's um, warm transfers specifically. Agent hits consult, talks to the second agent, then completes the transfer, and the pop is gone.

16
00:01:51.515 --> 00:01:55.716
Lena Brooks: Okay, that's helpful. Uh, Which Genesys version are you on now?

17
00:01:56.442 --> 00:02:09.674
Michelle Tran: Whatever they pushed on the third. I'd have to pull the exact build. Our platform team manages that. I think it's a 2026 release, but I don't have the number in front of me.

18
00:02:11.148 --> 00:02:18.559
Derek Shaw: I can grab that from the ticket. I think your platform contact listed it. Was it the cloud release or on-prem?

19
00:02:19.375 --> 00:02:22.843
Michelle Tran: Cloud. We moved off on-prem two years ago.

20
00:02:24.054 --> 00:02:24.854
Derek Shaw: Got it.

21
00:02:25.198 --> 00:02:30.245
Lena Brooks: And is this every queue, or specific ones? Sales queues, service queues, both?

22
00:02:30.938 --> 00:02:39.858
Michelle Tran: Sales, mostly. Our inbound sales queue. That's where we use PreSense the most. Service uses it less, so honestly I haven't tested service much.

23
00:02:40.295 --> 00:02:54.180
Brandon Hughes: Sales is the one that matters. That's the whole reason we bought it. If a caller clicks an ad, fills out a form, we want the agent to see that before they say hello.

24
00:02:54.945 --> 00:03:01.958
Lena Brooks: Right. So on the warm transfer, the second agent is in the same queue or a different one?

25
00:03:03.305 --> 00:03:12.542
Michelle Tran: Different. First agent is a general sales rep, then they consult with a specialist. Fiber specialist, wireless specialist, that kind of thing.

26
00:03:13.488 --> 00:03:18.265
Lena Brooks: And the specialist is the one who should get the pop?

27
00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:29.324
Michelle Tran: Yes. The specialist. The first agent already has the context, they're the one who um, took the call. The specialist is cold, they need the journey.

28
00:03:30.097 --> 00:03:39.481
Lena Brooks: Okay. And when you say the pop doesn't fire, is it that the panel loads empty, or the panel doesn't load at all?

29
00:03:40.526 --> 00:03:49.984
Michelle Tran: Doesn't load at all. The Genesys screen comes up with the normal stuff, contact record, notes, but the PreSense panel isn't there.

30
00:03:49.984 --> 00:03:52.993
Michelle Tran: It's like it never got the data.

31
00:03:53.846 --> 00:04:00.856
Derek Shaw: Do you have a recent call ID we can look at? Even one from this week?

32
00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:10.877
Michelle Tran: Yeah, I sent a few in the ticket. Um, Tuesday morning, I think three of them. I can send more if that helps.

33
00:04:11.286 --> 00:04:17.044
Derek Shaw: The three you sent are enough to start. I'll pull the signal logs on those.

34
00:04:17.377 --> 00:04:27.996
Brandon Hughes: How long does that take? Because we're three uh, weeks in and my team is manually pulling up the customer record before they pick up.

35
00:04:27.996 --> 00:04:34.791
Brandon Hughes: That's thirty, forty seconds per call. On a thousand calls a day, um, that's real money.

36
00:04:36.245 --> 00:04:48.212
Lena Brooks: I get it. That's a real cost. Let me ask one more thing. When the first agent consults, do they use the Genesys consult button, or do they do I mean, something else, like a conference?

37
00:04:48.412 --> 00:04:52.297
Michelle Tran: Consult button. Standard. That's how we've always done it.

38
00:04:52.697 --> 00:04:56.095
Lena Brooks: And it worked before the third? Same flow, same button?

39
00:04:57.198 --> 00:05:06.829
Michelle Tran: Yes. Nothing changed on our side. That's what's so frustrating. We didn't touch anything, they pushed an update, and now it's broken.

40
00:05:08.028 --> 00:05:25.736
Lena Brooks: Okay. So the most likely thing is something in how the transfer event gets surfaced to us changed. I don't want to guess at a root cause yet, but the pattern, warm transfer only, different queue, cloud version, points at the transfer signaling.

41
00:05:26.619 --> 00:05:34.716
Derek Shaw: That matches what I'm seeing in the logs. The PreSense payload is being built, I can see it on the first leg.

42
00:05:34.716 --> 00:05:38.397
Derek Shaw: It's the handoff to the second leg where it drops.

43
00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.163
Michelle Tran: So it's on your side?

44
00:05:41.867 --> 00:05:51.584
Derek Shaw: Not saying that. I'm saying the handoff is where the like, data stops. Whether that's us, Genesys, or the way the two are talking, I need to dig in.

45
00:05:51.584 --> 00:05:54.600
Derek Shaw: I don't want to point fingers before I know.

46
00:05:55.037 --> 00:06:00.098
Michelle Tran: Fine. But we need this fixed. My agents are complaining every day.

47
00:06:00.989 --> 00:06:11.969
Brandon Hughes: And it's not just the agents. I've got my VP asking whether we should even keep PreSense next year if it doesn't survive a transfer.

48
00:06:13.338 --> 00:06:21.791
Tom Lindqvist: Brandon, I hear that. Let's get to a test plan today so we're not sitting on this over the weekend.

49
00:06:22.493 --> 00:06:33.287
Lena Brooks: Yeah. Here's what I'd like to do. Michelle, can you get me the exact Genesys build number, and a screenshot of the specialist's screen on a warm transfer?

50
00:06:33.287 --> 00:06:36.371
Lena Brooks: Even a photo of the monitor is fine.

51
00:06:36.584 --> 00:06:46.471
Michelle Tran: I can get you the build number. The screenshot is trickier, we have a no-phone-on-the-floor policy, but I can grab a screen capture from the desktop.

52
00:06:47.842 --> 00:06:57.218
Lena Brooks: That works. And can you run a a controlled test? One call, warm transfer, same queue, then one warm transfer across queues.

53
00:06:57.218 --> 00:07:02.758
Lena Brooks: Note the time and the call IDs. That'll tell us if it's queue-specific.

54
00:07:04.214 --> 00:07:08.099
Michelle Tran: Okay. I can do that Monday morning when the floor's quieter.

55
00:07:09.340 --> 00:07:19.826
Derek Shaw: Send me uh, the call IDs as soon as you have them and I'll pull the logs in parallel. I'll also open a case with our platform team so it's tracked formally.

56
00:07:20.471 --> 00:07:24.600
Michelle Tran: Good. Because the ticket I opened has been sitting for a week.

57
00:07:25.685 --> 00:07:30.125
Derek Shaw: That's on us. I'll own it from here. You'll have my direct line.

58
00:07:30.601 --> 00:07:32.262
Brandon Hughes: What's the timeline? Realistically.

59
00:07:33.525 --> 00:07:42.120
Lena Brooks: Uh, if the test confirms what I think, we'll know by Wednesday. Fix depends on what we find. I'm not going to promise a date today.

60
00:07:42.854 --> 00:07:49.221
Brandon Hughes: I'd rather have a real answer than a fast one. Just keep us in the loop.

61
00:07:50.163 --> 00:07:55.928
Tom Lindqvist: I'll set up a check-in for Wednesday, same time. Michelle, Brandon, anything else before we wrap?

62
00:07:57.341 --> 00:08:00.727
Michelle Tran: No, that's it. Just, please, get this fixed.

63
00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:05.716
Tom Lindqvist: We're on it. And hey, good luck with the snow.

64
00:08:06.583 --> 00:08:07.673
Michelle Tran: Thanks. Talk Wednesday.

65
00:08:08.159 --> 00:08:08.959
Brandon Hughes: Thanks everyone.

66
00:08:10.246 --> 00:08:11.124
Lena Brooks: Thanks. Bye.

67
00:08:12.146 --> 00:08:12.946
Derek Shaw: Bye.
